  <h3 id="safe-specs">Specs the model wrote: <code>safe=True</code></h3>
  <p>
    A spec is data, and one written by a model or uploaded by a user should not be able to read
    arbitrary files or reach the network. Build it in safe mode: every file it names
    (<code>template</code>, a token <code>yaml</code>, an <code>image</code>, a
    <code>figure</code> path) must resolve inside <code>base_dir</code>, and HTML and Plotly
    figures are captured with network access blocked. A spec can never choose the browser
    executable, safe mode or not. Every content error is a <code>SpecError</code> — a
    <code>ValueError</code> — whose message and <code>.path</code> locate the bad value, which is
    exactly what to hand back to the model:
  </p>
  <Code code={safeSpec} />
  <p>
    See <a href={withBase("/advanced/compose")}>Compose &amp; Templates</a> for the full spec
    schema, and <a href={withBase("/advanced/lint")}>Lint &amp; Audit</a> for the lint gate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lint-cli">Checking a deck from the shell</h2>
  <p>
    When the agent hands back a <code>.pptx</code> rather than Python, <code>power-pptx lint</code>
    checks it without any code: <code>0</code> means nothing reached the <code>--fail-on</code>
    level, <code>1</code> that something did, <code>2</code> a usage error or unreadable file. The
    JSON report is the one to feed back into the agent's next turn.
  </p>
